# Destinations job containers may not reach, as fnmatch patterns matched
# against the request host.
#
# Denylist, not allowlist: agents legitimately browse the open web, so an
# allowlist would be unusable here. That is a real weakness worth stating —
# a denylist can be evaded by anything not on it, for instance a DNS name
# that resolves to a blocked address. The structural control (an internal
# network with no route off it) is what actually holds; this is the second
# layer that keeps holding if someone makes that network routable.

# --- cloud instance metadata ---
# The one that matters. Reaching this from untrusted code is how a sandbox
# becomes stolen IAM credentials.
169.254.169.254
169.254.170.2
metadata.google.internal
metadata.goog*

# --- link-local, the whole range ---
169.254.*

# --- RFC1918 private ranges: other services in the VPC ---
10.*
172.16.*
172.17.*
172.18.*
172.19.*
172.2*.*
172.30.*
172.31.*
192.168.*

# --- carrier-grade NAT, used by some cloud internals ---
100.64.*

# --- loopback: the proxy's own host ---
127.*
localhost
